WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:03.801
Picture an office.

2
00:00:03.801 --> 00:00:08.488
When it's time to go home, the manager writes it on the whiteboard by the door.

3
00:00:08.488 --> 00:00:12.759
Everyone glances at the board now and then, and leaves when it says yes.

4
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.836
A program can do the same.

5
00:00:14.836 --> 00:00:19.995
A worker thread, one path of work running beside others, loops through small jobs.

6
00:00:19.995 --> 00:00:23.392
Before each one, it checks a shared field called stop.

7
00:00:23.392 --> 00:00:26.475
One second in, the main thread sets stop to true.

8
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:29.721
Here's what the demo printed on JDK 25.

9
00:00:29.721 --> 00:00:31.444
Main set stop to true.

10
00:00:31.444 --> 00:00:35.282
Three seconds later, is the worker still running?

11
00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:35.674
True.

12
00:00:35.674 --> 00:00:36.849
It never stops.

13
00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:41.000
No crash, no error, just a busy processor doing laps.

14
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:44.817
So here's a question that feels almost too simple to ask.

15
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:45.851
Main wrote true.

16
00:00:45.851 --> 00:00:48.565
So where is the worker reading false from?

17
00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:50.504
Where does the old value live?

18
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.188
Most people answer: the cache.

19
00:00:53.188 --> 00:00:57.629
A core is the part of the processor that runs one thread at a time.

20
00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:03.329
Next to each core sits a cache, a small, fast memory that keeps copies of recent data.

21
00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:06.245
Main memory is about a hundred times slower.

22
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.323
So the story goes like this.

23
00:01:08.323 --> 00:01:14.094
The worker's core has an old copy of stop in its cache, and reads that copy forever.

24
00:01:14.094 --> 00:01:15.193
It sounds right.

25
00:01:15.193 --> 00:01:16.155
Let's test it.

26
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:26.315
Java has a switch, -Xint, that turns off the JIT compiler, the part of Java that turns busy code into fast machine code while the program runs.

27
00:01:26.315 --> 00:01:28.479
The caches don't change at all.

28
00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:32.527
So if the cache is to blame, the worker should still hang.

29
00:01:32.527 --> 00:01:33.085
Will it?

30
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:39.681
It stopped at once, after about 113 million laps.

31
00:01:39.681 --> 00:01:43.445
Same machine, same caches, and only the JIT changed.

32
00:01:43.445 --> 00:01:45.327
So the cache has an alibi.

33
00:01:45.327 --> 00:01:48.875
It was there in both runs, and only one run hung.

34
00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:54.067
To find the real place, let's go back to the office and look at the desks.

35
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:56.981
The whiteboard holds the numbers everyone shares.

36
00:01:56.981 --> 00:01:58.110
That's main memory.

37
00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:01.619
Each desk is a core, and the one working at it is a thread.

38
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:07.598
Walking to the wall is slow, so each desk keeps a photocopy of the part of the board it uses.

39
00:02:07.598 --> 00:02:09.344
That photocopy is the cache.

40
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:11.610
And the office has one strict rule.

41
00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:16.830
When anyone changes a number on the board, every photocopy of it is crossed out at once.

42
00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.025
The next look goes back to the board.

43
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.711
Processors follow that rule in hardware.

44
00:02:21.711 --> 00:02:27.046
It's called cache coherence, and it works within nanoseconds, billionths of a second.

45
00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:29.055
So a photocopy can't stay wrong.

46
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.874
But there is one place where a write can wait.

47
00:02:31.874 --> 00:02:33.560
Each desk has an outbox tray.

48
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:39.663
When a clerk has a new number for the board, she drops a note in her tray and goes on with her next task.

49
00:02:39.663 --> 00:02:42.453
A runner carries it to the board a moment later.

50
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:51.183
That tray is called a store buffer: a small queue inside each core where writes wait on their way to the cache, so the core never stands around.

51
00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:52.334
Notice the catch.

52
00:02:52.334 --> 00:02:57.752
If her next task is to read a different number, she may read it before her own note arrives.

53
00:02:57.752 --> 00:03:00.932
To everyone else, her write and her read look swapped.

54
00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:04.650
Let's catch that with the smallest test there is.

55
00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.357
Two fields, x and y, both start at 0.

56
00:03:07.357 --> 00:03:10.209
Thread A writes 1 into x, then reads y.

57
00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:13.061
Thread B writes 1 into y, then reads x.

58
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.148
Play it by hand first.

59
00:03:15.148 --> 00:03:22.928
Shuffle the four steps any way you like, but keep each thread's two steps in order, like shuffling two decks of cards together.

60
00:03:22.928 --> 00:03:26.052
Whoever reads second reads after the other's write.

61
00:03:26.052 --> 00:03:28.012
So at least one thread sees a 1.

62
00:03:28.012 --> 00:03:30.217
Both reading 0 should be impossible.

63
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.958
The demo runs this test 524,288 times.

64
00:03:34.958 --> 00:03:39.333
In how many trials do both threads read 0?

65
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.440
Run one: 240,989.

66
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.480
Run two: 225,261.

67
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.761
Run three: 171,281.

68
00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:54.041
That's a third to nearly half of all trials.

69
00:03:54.041 --> 00:03:59.201
For something impossible, it happens a lot.

70
00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:01.289
Here's how.

71
00:04:01.289 --> 00:04:07.404
Thread A drops its note for x in its tray, and Thread B drops its note for y in its tray.

72
00:04:07.404 --> 00:04:11.183
Then each one reads the other field from its photocopy.

73
00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:12.214
Zero, and zero.

74
00:04:12.214 --> 00:04:15.031
Only then do the runners reach the board.

75
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:23.350
Turn the JIT off again, and the count drops, but stays well above zero: 44,000 to 110,000 in our runs.

76
00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.385
The interpreter doesn't reorder anything.

77
00:04:26.385 --> 00:04:29.345
So this time, it really is the hardware.

78
00:04:29.533 --> 00:04:35.441
Now make both fields volatile, a keyword that marks a field as shared between threads.

79
00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.295
The count is 0, every time.

80
00:04:37.295 --> 00:04:46.705
On x86 chips, the kind in most laptops, Java follows each volatile write with an instruction that empties the tray before any later read.

81
00:04:46.867 --> 00:04:49.494
So the tray can swap a write and a read.

82
00:04:49.494 --> 00:04:52.319
But it always delivers, within nanoseconds.

83
00:04:52.319 --> 00:04:54.355
It can't hold stop for an hour.

84
00:04:54.355 --> 00:04:56.195
The outbox has an alibi too.

85
00:04:56.933 --> 00:04:59.760
There's one more thing on each desk: a notepad.

86
00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:06.738
To work with a number, a clerk copies it onto her notepad, because a glance down is quicker than a walk to the wall.

87
00:05:06.738 --> 00:05:10.948
The notepad has no crossing-out rule, and nobody else can write on it.

88
00:05:11.733 --> 00:05:13.386
Now here's what the JIT did.

89
00:05:13.386 --> 00:05:17.046
Once the loop had run many thousands of times, it compiled it.

90
00:05:17.046 --> 00:05:21.178
It saw that nothing in the loop writes stop or talks to other threads.

91
00:05:21.178 --> 00:05:25.605
So it read stop once, before the loop, and turned the rest into while true.

92
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:34.343
In the office, it's as if the clerk copied the answer, no, onto her notepad once, and from then on reads only the notepad.

93
00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:35.704
Main writes true.

94
00:05:35.704 --> 00:05:37.427
The note leaves the tray.

95
00:05:37.427 --> 00:05:38.804
The board says true.

96
00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.596
Both photocopies say true.

97
00:05:40.596 --> 00:05:43.420
And the worker keeps reading its notepad.

98
00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:50.136
The notepad is a register, a tiny storage slot inside a core, the fastest place a value can live.

99
00:05:50.136 --> 00:05:52.360
That's where the old value lives.

100
00:05:52.533 --> 00:05:55.256
It's the most focused worker in the building.

101
00:05:55.256 --> 00:05:57.736
It never looks up, so it never goes home.

102
00:05:57.736 --> 00:06:03.604
Jokes aside, the compiled loop never reads memory again, so no rule in the hardware can reach it.

103
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:06.019
So here's the office as it stands.

104
00:06:06.019 --> 00:06:09.412
The photocopy, the cache, agrees within nanoseconds.

105
00:06:09.412 --> 00:06:14.762
The outbox, the store buffer, holds a write for nanoseconds, then always delivers.

106
00:06:14.762 --> 00:06:20.765
The notepad, the register, can hold an old value forever, but only because the JIT chose it.

107
00:06:20.933 --> 00:06:23.495
And here's where the office picture breaks.

108
00:06:23.495 --> 00:06:26.354
A clerk decides where to look, glance by glance.

109
00:06:26.354 --> 00:06:32.311
The JIT decides once, when it compiles the loop, and the core repeats that choice billions of times.

110
00:06:32.311 --> 00:06:34.336
That's why the hang lasts forever.

111
00:06:35.133 --> 00:06:35.957
One warning.

112
00:06:35.957 --> 00:06:38.358
Chips differ in how much they swap.

113
00:06:38.358 --> 00:06:43.091
x86 chips only let a later read pass an earlier write, like our test.

114
00:06:43.091 --> 00:06:49.197
ARM chips, in phones and many servers, also let reads pass reads, and writes pass writes.

115
00:06:49.333 --> 00:06:53.344
So code that never fails on your laptop can fail on a server.

116
00:06:53.344 --> 00:06:57.354
A clean run on your laptop only proves your laptop is polite.

117
00:06:57.354 --> 00:07:00.839
Write to Java's rules instead: the Java Memory Model.

118
00:07:00.839 --> 00:07:07.216
It promises visibility, one thread seeing another's write, only when something links the threads.

119
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.912
For our flag, the link is one word: volatile.

120
00:07:10.912 --> 00:07:16.282
The JIT may not keep a volatile field on the notepad, so every pass reads it again.

121
00:07:16.282 --> 00:07:20.035
The photocopy says true, and the worker finally goes home.

122
00:07:20.035 --> 00:07:24.176
And Java promises that main's write becomes visible to the loop.

123
00:07:24.933 --> 00:07:28.345
Here it is, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

124
00:07:28.345 --> 00:07:34.461
In Kotlin, the annotation @Volatile marks the field volatile, so the compiled code is the same.

125
00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.463
So, where does the old value live?

126
00:07:37.463 --> 00:07:41.389
Not in the cache: the photocopies agree within nanoseconds.

127
00:07:41.389 --> 00:07:44.982
Not in the outbox: it delivers within nanoseconds too.

128
00:07:44.982 --> 00:07:50.106
It lives on the notepad, a register, because the JIT chose to read stop once.

129
00:07:50.106 --> 00:07:52.236
volatile takes the notepad away.

130
00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.227
Quick check.

131
00:07:53.227 --> 00:07:57.157
A loop hangs with the JIT on, and stops with the JIT off.

132
00:07:57.157 --> 00:07:59.225
Photocopy, outbox, or notepad?
